      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-91033",
        "knowledge_point_id": "ec3823db-6df8-502a-a050-b5aa169d2e9d",
        "knowledge_point": "Applying Differential Operators With Constant Coefficients",
        "problem": "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$",
        "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=-2,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
